                <pb facs="0778" n="749" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76v11/pg749"/>

                <head>272. Backchannel Message From the Presidentʼs Assistant for National Security
                    Affairs (Kissinger) to the Ambassador to Pakistan (Farland)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d272-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1971-12-10">December 10, 1971</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Ref: [<hi rendition="#italic">message number not declassified</hi>]<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d272-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>We are making strongest démarche to Soviets today which proposes that they join
                    with us in supporting provisos contained in my message of December 9<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d272-f3"/> and paragraph 3 of your [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >message number not declassified</hi>] which provides (a) India and Pakistan
                    should agree to an immediate ceasefire with the separate armed forces “standing
                    fast”; and that the United Nations or other international organization provide
                    observers to see that the ceasefire is effective; (b) that India and Pakistan
                    “at any effective level” immediately open negotiations aimed at a settlement of
                    the war and troop withdrawal; and coincident therewith negotiations be started
                    looking towards the political satisfaction of Bengali aspirations; i.e., a
                    political settlement.</p>
                <pb facs="0779" n="750" xml:id="pg750"/>
                <p>It is essential that Government of Pakistan refrain from making additional
                    proposals until we have had opportunity to move within the above framework.</p>
                <p>In discussing the foregoing procedure with Yahya, you should emphasize that the
                    President has made the strongest démarche to the Soviets and included warning to
                    them that we have obligations towards Pakistan which will not permit aggression
                    against West Pakistan. President added that should Indian offensive be launched
                    in the West, with Soviet acquiescence, a US/Soviet confrontation would
                    ensue.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
